%[text] %[text:anchor:T_0B83894C] # Design Bi-Directional CLLC Resonant Converter
%[text] This design solution shows how to design and analyze a bi-directional CLLC resonant DC-DC converter. The CLLC resonant converter is a soft-switching topology with turn-on zero-voltage switching (ZVS) for the primary MOSFETs and zero-current switching (ZCS) for the secondary MOSFETs. Its bi-directional power flow suits battery charging and discharging. The topology gives high power density, low switching losses, and low electromagnetic interference (EMI).
%[text] This design solution shows how to estimate and tune the resonant tank parameters to meet your design requirements. It extracts the frequency response from a time-domain simulation with sinusoidal disturbances, which gives a linearized plant model for controller tuning.
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A6556DB6] ## Design Solution Scope
%[text] This design solution takes converter specifications to a validated design. It uses the first harmonic approximation (FHA) to estimate the resonant tank parameters and plots the gain curve for various load resistances, quality factors, and ratios of magnetizing inductance to series resonant inductance. It also linearizes the converter, tunes the controller, reports the switching and conduction losses of each semiconductor device, and estimates the efficiency.
%[text] In this design solution, you:
%[text] - Estimate resonant tank parameters
%[text] - Plot the gain curve using the FHA
%[text] - Tune resonant tank parameters
%[text] - Linearize the plant and tune the controller
%[text] - Estimate power loss and efficiency \
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_B6482FCD] ## System Overview
%[text] The CLLC resonant full-bridge DC-DC converter model includes four key components:
%[text] - Full-bridge inverter with SiC MOSFET devices
%[text] - Resonant tank comprising a capacitor and a transformer with specified leakage and magnetizing inductance
%[text] - Full-bridge synchronous rectifier with MOSFET devices
%[text] - CLLC controller  \
%[text] This figure shows the top-level schematic of the CLLC converter. The converter uses the transformer leakage inductance for resonance and a full-bridge MOSFET synchronous rectifier on the secondary side, which lets power flow in either direction.
%[text] Open the model 
  %[control:button:6d7c]{"position":[1,2]}
modelName = "CLLCResonantConverter";
open_system(modelName);
%[text] ![](text:image:8562)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_72e5] ## Select CLLC Resonant Tank Parameters
%[text] To design and tune the resonant tank, use the `EstimateCLLCConverterResonantTankParameters` live script. You specify your design requirements and configure the resonant tank, and the script plots the gain curve of the resonant tank using the FHA.
%[text] To open the parameter estimation workflow, enter this command in the MATLAB® Command Window:
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] edit 'EstimateCLLCConverterResonantTankParameters'
%[text] ```
if ~exist('CLLCDesign', 'var');CLLCConverterDefaultInputParameters; end
disp(table(designTable,'VariableNames',"CLLC Converter Estimated Design Parameters")); %[output:53a685d5]
%[text] ## Select Converter Components
%[text] This design solution helps you select the ratings of the primary and secondary bridge MOSFETs and pick catalog parts that meet them.
%[text] To open the component selection workflow, enter this command in the MATLAB Command Window:
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] edit 'SelectCLLCConverterComponents'
%[text] ```
%[text] ## Design the Controller
%[text] A separate live script describes the controller options and the design of the output voltage loop. The tuning workflow estimates the plant poles and zeros from a simulated frequency response and computes the compensator coefficients from them.
%[text] To open the controller design workflow, run:
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] edit 'CLLCResonantConverterControllerDesign'
%[text] ```
%[text] To open the controller tuning workflow, run:
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] edit 'TuneCLLCResonantConverter'
%[text] ```
%%
%[text] ## CLLC Resonant Converter Circuit
%[text] The CLLC resonant converter plant consists of the primary full-bridge converter with MOSFETs, the resonant tank, and the secondary full-bridge converter. The **Model option** parameter selects `Simplified`, which has no switching devices and simulates fastest, or `Detailed`, which switches Simscape™ Electrical™ device blocks. For the `Detailed` power stage, you select the device model of each bridge: `Ideal Switch` for faster simulation during controller tuning, or `MOSFET (Ideal, Switching) with Thermal` to estimate switching losses and efficiency.
%[text] To open the CLLC resonant converter circuit subsystem, at the MATLAB Command Window, run:
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] open_system(strcat(modelName,"/CLLC Converter Power Circuit"));
%[text] ```
%[text] ![](text:image:5b4b)
%[text] 
%[text] ## Plot Simulation Results
%[text] Simulate the CLLC resonant converter at different reference voltages and loads, then analyze the current and voltage waveforms and the losses.
%%
%[text] ### Set up Simulation
%[text] Set the input DC voltage, the output reference voltage, the load power, and the simulation time. The loss and efficiency section repeats this operating point so that you can run it on its own.
  %[control:button:4fef]{"position":[1,2]}
voltageReference = simscape.Value(370,"V");   % Output reference voltage (V)
inputDCVoltage   = simscape.Value(390,"V");   % Input DC voltage (V)
outputLoadPower  = simscape.Value(7000,"W");  % DC output power (W)
simulationTime   = simscape.Value(0.015,"s"); % Simulation time (s)
% Set the simulation time so that the output voltage reaches steady state. On the Simplified
% power stage used for the waveform plots, the output rises to the reference in about 11 ms.
%[text] ### Plot CLLC Converter Voltage and Current (IV Curves)
%[text] To plot the converter waveforms at the operating point, use `getConverterWaveforms`. It simulates the `Simplified` power stage and plots the primary and secondary AC voltages and currents, the input and output DC currents and the output DC voltage. To set the number of resonant-frequency cycles plotted, use `FocusCycles`.
cllcIVData = getConverterWaveforms(modelName, Converter="CLLC", ... %[output:group:4ec5a308] %[output:05039db1]
    Vref=voltageReference, Vin=inputDCVoltage, Pload=outputLoadPower, ... %[output:05039db1]
    SimTime=simulationTime, ... %[output:05039db1]
    FrequencyHz=CLLCSpec.resonantFrequency.value, FocusCycles=5); %[output:group:4ec5a308] %[output:05039db1]
%%
%[text] ### Estimate Power Loss and Efficiency
%[text] To estimate the switching and conduction losses and the efficiency, use `getConverterPowerLoss`. It simulates the plant with the `MOSFET (Ideal, Switching) with Thermal` device option.
%[text] By default, the losses are averaged over the last 20% of the simulation, after the startup transient. To change the window, set `AveragingWindow`. To split the switching loss into turn-on, turn-off, and reverse-recovery components, set `SwitchingLossDetail="separated"`.
  %[control:button:55d7]{"position":[1,2]}
modelName = "CLLCResonantConverter";
voltageReference = simscape.Value(370,"V");   % Reference voltage (V)
inputDCVoltage   = simscape.Value(390,"V");   % Input DC voltage (V)
outputLoadPower  = simscape.Value(7000,"W");  % DC output power (W)
simulationTime   = simscape.Value(0.02,"s");  % Simulation time (s)
% Set the simulation time so that the output voltage reaches steady state. 
%[text] The CLLC resonant converter has low switching losses because it achieves ZVS in the primary MOSFETs and ZCS in the secondary MOSFETs over most of the load and voltage range. The losses fall into three groups: primary bridge MOSFET losses, secondary bridge MOSFET losses, and passive component losses such as transformer winding resistance.
lossData = getConverterPowerLoss(modelName, Converter="CLLC", ... %[output:group:76207f13] %[output:19eec07a] %[output:77764782]
    Vref=voltageReference, Vin=inputDCVoltage, Pload=outputLoadPower, ... %[output:19eec07a] %[output:77764782]
    SimTime=simulationTime, SwitchingLossDetail="lumped"); %[output:group:76207f13] %[output:19eec07a] %[output:77764782]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_7A3E9C04] ## Conclusion
%[text] This design solution shows how to use MATLAB® scripting and Simscape Electrical to design a CLLC resonant full-bridge converter. It sizes the key passive components to meet the converter requirements, models the circuit at low fidelity for fast simulation and control design, and models it in enough detail to predict losses and efficiency. For more information on CLLC resonant converter design, see the references.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_73eb] ## References
%[text] 1. Sam Abdel-Rahman, "Resonant CLLC Converter:Operation and Design 250W, 33Vin, 400Vout, Design Example", Infineon Technologies North America (IFNA) Corp.
%[text] 2. P. P. Gupta, N. Kumar and U. Nangia, "Design and Analysis of CLLC Resonant Converter and CCCV Topology for Battery Charging," *2022 2nd Asian Conference on Innovation in Technology (ASIANCON)*, Ravet, India, 2022, pp. 1-5, doi: 10.1109/ ASIANCON55314.2022.9909135.
%[text] 3. Christophe Basso, "Control Methods of CLLC Converters", Future Electronics, 12th Power Analysis and Design Symposium.
%[text] 4. B. Li, X. Zhang, D. Zhou, S. Sun, Y. Wang and D. Xu, "Soft-switching Characteristics Analysis Based on CLLC Resonant Converter," *2018 IEEE Transportation Electrification Conference and Expo, Asia-Pacific (ITEC Asia-Pacific)*, Bangkok, Thailand, 2018, pp. 1-6, doi: 10.1109/ITEC-AP.2018.8433301.
%[text] 5. Dhaval Patel and Ramesh Kankanala, "Digital Compensator Design for CLLC Resonant Converter", Application note (AN1477), Microchip Technology Inc. \
